#: qt/src/EmulationPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>A hack option. Only use this if you are running a third-party hack of a game that was designed for emulators and needs this.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>A hack option. Only use this if you are running a third-party hack of a game that was designed for emulators and needs this.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/EmulationPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Allows more sprites to be displayed on the screen in an attempt to remove flickering.</p><p>Some games take advantage of this limit and use it to hide glitches, and removing the limit will make those visible.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Allows more sprites to be displayed on the screen in an attempt to remove flickering.</p><p>Some games take advantage of this limit and use it to hide glitches, and removing the limit will make those visible.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/EmulationPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Alternate sound filters can make the audio more crisp than the authentic gaussian filter.</p><p>The Cubic and Sinc filters provide the best improvement, but they can reveal some noise that would normally be hidden by the hardware.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Alternate sound filters can make the audio more crisp than the authentic gaussian filter.</p><p>The Cubic and Sinc filters provide the best improvement, but they can reveal some noise that would normally be hidden by the hardware.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/EmulationPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Amount of memory to use to enable the rewind function. The amount of time each MB gets you varies based on the complexity of the game. Set to 0 to disable rewind altogether and use less processor time.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Amount of memory to use to enable the rewind function. The amount of time each MB gets you varies based on the complexity of the game. Set to 0 to disable rewind altogether and use less processor time.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/EmulationPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Change the speed of the SuperFX chip. The SuperFX had some notoriously slow games, and this can be used to attempt to speed them up.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Change the speed of the SuperFX chip. The SuperFX had some notoriously slow games, and this can be used to attempt to speed them up.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Choose one of <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Qt Software, Vulkan, or OpenGL</span>.</p><p>Qt Software will use the CPU to draw the display. Vulkan or OpenGL will use that respective graphics interface to draw the display.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Choose one of <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Qt Software, Vulkan, or OpenGL</span>.</p><p>Qt Software will use the CPU to draw the display. Vulkan or OpenGL will use that respective graphics interface to draw the display.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/ControllerPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Clear or swap sets of bindings between controllers.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Clear or swap sets of bindings between controllers.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Control how much the audio is stretched initially.</p><p><br/></p><p>Unless you know what you're doing, it's best to use the option above this to adjust it automatically.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Control how much the audio is stretched initially.</p><p><br/></p><p>Unless you know what you're doing, it's best to use the option above this to adjust it automatically.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/FoldersPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Determines where to look for optional BIOS / boot ROM files (Super Game Boy boot ROM, BS-X.bin, etc).</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">ROM Folder</span> looks next to the loaded ROM.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Config Folder</span> uses a \"bios\" subfolder of the config folder.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Custom</span> uses an explicit path (default: \"Bios\" relative to the working directory).</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Determines where to look for optional BIOS / boot ROM files (Super Game Boy boot ROM, BS-X.bin, etc).</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">ROM Folder</span> looks next to the loaded ROM.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Config Folder</span> uses a \"bios\" subfolder of the config folder.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Custom</span> uses an explicit path (default: \"Bios\" relative to the working directory).</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/FoldersPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Determines where to save these files.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">ROM Folder</span> will always save to the same folder as the ROM.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Config Folder</span> will save to a subfolder of the config folder that is shown. If there is a snes9x.conf file next to your executable, Snes9x will operate in portable mode and the config folder will be the folder the executable is in.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Custom</span> allows you to specify exactly where to save these files.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Determines where to save these files.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">ROM Folder</span> will always save to the same folder as the ROM.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Config Folder</span> will save to a subfolder of the config folder that is shown. If there is a snes9x.conf file next to your executable, Snes9x will operate in portable mode and the config folder will be the folder the executable is in.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Custom</span> allows you to specify exactly where to save these files.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Erases all emulated sound output and just sends out silence.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Erases all emulated sound output and just sends out silence.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>If after everything, you're still getting pops and crackles in audio, you can increase this value. However, it may cause noticeable distortion.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>If after everything, you're still getting pops and crackles in audio, you can increase this value. However, it may cause noticeable distortion.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Master output volume during normal play. Moving this also drags the per-source SGB sliders so they stay in step.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Master output volume during normal play. Moving this also drags the per-source SGB sliders so they stay in step.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Master output volume during turbo and rewind.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Master output volume during turbo and rewind.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Mutes sound when using the turbo and rewind functions, which might be annoying to some users.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Mutes sound when using the turbo and rewind functions, which might be annoying to some users.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>On classic CRT televisions, some areas of the screen were covered by the bezel, so weren't used often in games. This option allows you to show that area, but it will usually be just black bars.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>On classic CRT televisions, some areas of the screen were covered by the bezel, so weren't used often in games. This option allows you to show that area, but it will usually be just black bars.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/EmulationPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Original controllers may have had a small pin in the dpad preventing left and right or up and down from being pressed at the same time. This will allow that behavior if you're using a keyboard or device that can press those buttons at the same time. Some games will glitch when this is done.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Original controllers may have had a small pin in the dpad preventing left and right or up and down from being pressed at the same time. This will allow that behaviour if you're using a keyboard or device that can press those buttons at the same time. Some games will glitch when this is done.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/EmulationPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Override the frame rate of the emulated system. This can be used to run slightly faster or slower than usual, or to match the timer to your exact refresh rate.</p><p><br/></p><p>Set this to 0 to use whatever the emulated system uses.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Override the frame rate of the emulated system. This can be used to run slightly faster or slower than usual, or to match the timer to your exact refresh rate.</p><p><br/></p><p>Set this to 0 to use whatever the emulated system uses.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Selects the driver to output sound with.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Cubeb: </span>The recommended option.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">SDL</span>: If Cubeb doesn't work.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">PulseAudio:</span> Unix only. May have less latency.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Selects the driver to output sound with.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Cubeb: </span>The recommended option.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">SDL</span>: If Cubeb doesn't work.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">PulseAudio:</span> Unix only. May have less latency.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/FoldersPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Setting this to anything other than 0 will save the SRAM (internal game saves) file regularly. If it is set to to 0, it will only save when exiting Snes9x or changing games, and you will lose any progress since the last save if your device crashes.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Setting this to anything other than 0 will save the SRAM (internal game saves) file regularly. If it is set to to 0, it will only save when exiting Snes9x or changing games, and you will lose any progress since the last save if your device crashes.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>SGB BIOS mix: GB channel volume. Active in SGB1/SGB2 BIOS mode; in BIOS-less mode this mirrors the Regular master, which is what actually scales GB output.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>SGB BIOS mix: GB channel volume. Active in SGB1/SGB2 BIOS mode; in BIOS-less mode this mirrors the Regular master, which is what actually scales GB output.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>SGB BIOS mix: SPC channel volume. Only active in SGB1/SGB2 BIOS mode — BIOS-less GB playback discards SPC output.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>SGB BIOS mix: SPC channel volume. Only active in SGB1/SGB2 BIOS mode — BIOS-less GB playback discards SPC output.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/EmulationPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Skips rendering the specified amount of frames when using the fast-forward function. The higher this amount, the faster it goes.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Skips rendering the specified amount of frames when using the fast-forward function. The higher this amount, the faster it goes.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>The buffer size for the emulator side of sound playback. Ideally, it should be about two frames worth of audio, or about 32ms at 60Hz. Latency will be half this value.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>The buffer size for the emulator side of sound playback. Ideally, it should be about two frames worth of audio, or about 32ms at 60Hz. Latency will be half this value.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/ControllerPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>The enumerated controller as seen by the SNES. Controller 1 will always be quot;Player 1quot; and so on, no matter what device is assigned to the controller ports.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>The enumerated controller as seen by the SNES. Controller 1 will always be quot;Player 1quot; and so on, no matter what device is assigned to the controller ports.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/EmulationPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>The number of frames between each rewind snapshot. More frames mean rewind can go back further and will rewind faster. </p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>The number of frames between each rewind snapshot. More frames mean rewind can go back further and will rewind faster. </p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>The output playback rate on your computer. This is <span style=\" font-weight:700;\">not</span> the playback rate of the emulated system.</p><p>You should set this to match your operating system's sound server. It should almost always be 48000Hz.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>The output playback rate on your computer. This is <span style=\" font-weight:700;\">not</span> the playback rate of the emulated system.</p><p>You should set this to match your operating system's sound server. It should almost always be 48000Hz.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/ShortcutsPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This box lists application shortcuts. There are 4 slots, so you can assign 4 different keys or buttons to one function.</p><p>To set a binding, click a box or highlight it with the keyboard and press Enter/Return. The box will show three dots quot;...quot;. Press the desired key or gamepad button to assign it to that box. The Escape key will clear the box.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This box lists application shortcuts. There are 4 slots, so you can assign 4 different keys or buttons to one function.</p><p>To set a binding, click a box or highlight it with the keyboard and press Enter/Return. The box will show three dots quot;...quot;. Press the desired key or gamepad button to assign it to that box. The Escape key will clear the box.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/ControllerPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This box lists keyboard keys and gamepad buttons mapped to the SNES controller. There are 4 slots, so you can assign 4 different keys or buttons to one SNES button.</p><p>To set a binding, click a box or highlight it with the keyboard and press Enter/Return. The box will show three dots quot;...quot;. Press the desired key or gamepad button to assign it to that box. The Escape key will clear the box.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This box lists keyboard keys and gamepad buttons mapped to the SNES controller. There are 4 slots, so you can assign 4 different keys or buttons to one SNES button.</p><p>To set a binding, click a box or highlight it with the keyboard and press Enter/Return. The box will show three dots quot;...quot;. Press the desired key or gamepad button to assign it to that box. The Escape key will clear the box.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/EmulationPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This controls how the program runs the emulated content at the correct speed.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Timer</span>: This sets a literal timer and shows emulated frames at the exact frame rate specified, or automatically matches the emulated system. It may not match your display's frame rate, which can cause judders.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Timer with Frameskipping</span>: The same as timer, except if your display has a lower refresh rate than the emulated system it will skip frames to keep up.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Sound Synchronization: </span>Assume the sound engine is outputting the sound at the correct speed and match the video output rate with it. This assumes your refresh rate is fairly close to the emulated system and you have vsync turned on.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">None:</span> Don't attempt to slow emulation down. If you turn off vsync, this will run as fast as possible. If your display matches the emulated system's refresh rate closely, this with vsync enabled is generally the smoothest option aside from variable refresh rates with a timer.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This controls how the program runs the emulated content at the correct speed.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Timer</span>: This sets a literal timer and shows emulated frames at the exact frame rate specified, or automatically matches the emulated system. It may not match your display's frame rate, which can cause judders.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Timer with Frameskipping</span>: The same as timer, except if your display has a lower refresh rate than the emulated system it will skip frames to keep up.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Sound Synchronization: </span>Assume the sound engine is outputting the sound at the correct speed and match the video output rate with it. This assumes your refresh rate is fairly close to the emulated system and you have vsync turned on.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">None:</span> Don't attempt to slow emulation down. If you turn off vsync, this will run as fast as possible. If your display matches the emulated system's refresh rate closely, this with vsync enabled is generally the smoothest option aside from variable refresh rates with a timer.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This option adapts <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">maintain aspect ratio</span> and scales the image in steps of integral scale, so game pixels are never spread across the borders of screen pixels.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This option adapts <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">maintain aspect ratio</span> and scales the image in steps of integral scale, so game pixels are never spread across the borders of screen pixels.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This option affects how <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">maintain aspect ratio </span>works. </p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">4:3 </span>will maintain a ratio where the screen is 1.333 times wider than high.</p><p>64:49 presents an idealized ratio where the display output is perfect, but is affected by NTSC compression.</p><p>1:1 Means pixels will be square. This will squeeze the image horizontally.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This option affects how <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">maintain aspect ratio </span>works. </p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">4:3 </span>will maintain a ratio where the screen is 1.333 times wider than high.</p><p>64:49 presents an idealized ratio where the display output is perfect, but is affected by NTSC compression.</p><p>1:1 Means pixels will be square. This will squeeze the image horizontally.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This option causes the audio to be slightly stretched to keep the latency even and to prevent underruns and overruns, which will cause pops and cracks in the audio.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This option causes the audio to be slightly stretched to keep the latency even and to prevent underruns and overruns, which will cause pops and cracks in the audio.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This option determines whether messages show up in the game display:</p><p>Onscreen will show a high resolution overlay when using <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Vulkan</span> or <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">OpenGL</span>.</p><p>Inscreen will draw in a pixel font to the ingame display.</p><p>Don't display messages will show nothing.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This option determines whether messages show up in the game display:</p><p>Onscreen will show a high resolution overlay when using <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Vulkan</span> or <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">OpenGL</span>.</p><p>Inscreen will draw in a pixel font to the ingame display.</p><p>Don't display messages will show nothing.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/EmulationPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This option is explicitly for old AddMusic hacks. If you know you have one of those, you can enable this. Otherwise, leave it unchecked.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This option is explicitly for old AddMusic hacks. If you know you have one of those, you can enable this. Otherwise, leave it unchecked.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This option keeps the proportions of the emulated display the same no matter how you change the size of the window.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This option keeps the proportions of the emulated display the same no matter how you change the size of the window.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This option only affects the size of the font and the icons shown when display messages are set to the <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Onscreen</span> option.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This option only affects the size of the font and the icons shown when display messages are set to the <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Onscreen</span> option.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This option only affects the SNES's high-resolution, 512 pixel wide mode. Few games use this. </p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Output directly</span> will use whatever is output directly, but when the game changes modes, the filtering will get sharper or blurrier and may be distracting.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Merge fields </span>will scale the image down and blend the extra pixels together. This is useful in games like Jurassic Park and Kirby's Dreamland 3, which use it for a transparency effect.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Scale low resolution </span>will double the width of low-resolution screens, so when the game switches between modes there isn't any distracting changes in filtering. This is useful when a game uses the high resolution mode often, like Seiken Densetsu 3.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This option only affects the SNES's high-resolution, 512 pixel wide mode. Few games use this. </p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Output directly</span> will use whatever is output directly, but when the game changes modes, the filtering will get sharper or blurrier and may be distracting.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Merge fields </span>will scale the image down and blend the extra pixels together. This is useful in games like Jurassic Park and Kirby's Dreamland 3, which use it for a transparency effect.</p><p><span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Scale low resolution </span>will double the width of low-resolution screens, so when the game switches between modes there isn't any distracting changes in filtering. This is useful when a game uses the high resolution mode often, like Seiken Densetsu 3.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This option prevents tearing in the output.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This option prevents tearing in the output.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/SoundPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This option stretches the emulated audio output to coincide exactly with your display's refresh rate. It automatically handles the <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Input Rate</span> option.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This option stretches the emulated audio output to coincide exactly with your display's refresh rate. It automatically handles the <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Input Rate</span> option.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/ControllerPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This option will use the SDL library to auto-configure gamepads. It will take the XBox mappings for the left D-pad and analog stick, as well as the the face buttons and L/R shoulder buttons, and assign them to relative locations on the first SNES controller.</p><p><br/></p><p>All gamepads will be assigned to player 1. If you need more players, disable this option and set up the mappings manually.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This option will use the SDL library to auto-configure gamepads. It will take the XBox mappings for the left D-pad and analog stick, as well as the the face buttons and L/R shoulder buttons, and assign them to relative locations on the first SNES controller.</p><p><br/></p><p>All gamepads will be assigned to player 1. If you need more players, disable this option and set up the mappings manually.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>This prevents the GPU from queuing up additional frames in advance, reducing the time it takes for the display to react to your input.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>This prevents the GPU from queuing up additional frames in advance, reducing the time it takes for the display to react to your input.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Turns on a bilinear smoothing filter to smooth out the pixels. This option won't work with a hardware shader enabled.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Turns on a bilinear smoothing filter to smooth out the pixels. This option won't work with a hardware shader enabled.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/ControllerPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Use this to automatically assign the default keyboard mapping or a detected controller to a set of bindings.</p><p>This uses the XInput controller protocol and maps it as close as possible to a SNES controller.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Use this to automatically assign the default keyboard mapping or a detected controller to a set of bindings.</p><p>This uses the XInput controller protocol and maps it as close as possible to a SNES controller.</p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>Uses a hardware shader with <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Vulkan</span> or <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">OpenGL</span> drivers. These can be either .glslp or .slangp preset files.</p><p>GLSL shaders (.glslp) can be downloaded from:</p><p><a href=\"https://github.com/libretro/glsl-shaders\"><span style=\" text-decoration: underline; color:#2980b9;\">https://github.com/libretro/glsl-shaders</span></a></p><p>Slang shaders (.slangp) can be downloaded from:</p><p><a href=\"https://github.com/libretro/slang-shaders\"><span style=\" text-decoration: underline; color:#2980b9;\">https://github.com/libretro/slang-shaders</span></a></p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>Uses a hardware shader with <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">Vulkan</span> or <span style=\" font-style:italic;\">OpenGL</span> drivers. These can be either .glslp or .slangp preset files.</p><p>GLSL shaders (.glslp) can be downloaded from:</p><p><a href=\"https://github.com/libretro/glsl-shaders\"><span style=\" text-decoration: underline; color:#2980b9;\">https://github.com/libretro/glsl-shaders</span></a></p><p>Slang shaders (.slangp) can be downloaded from:</p><p><a href=\"https://github.com/libretro/slang-shaders\"><span style=\" text-decoration: underline; color:#2980b9;\">https://github.com/libretro/slang-shaders</span></a></p></body></html>"

#: qt/src/DisplayPanel.ui
msgid "<html><head/><body><p>When entering fullscreen mode, this option temporarily sets a number of other options to values that make variable refresh rate monitors that use FreeSync or GSync smoother.</p><p>Enables vsync.</p><p>Sets synchronization mode to timer and the frame rate to exactly that of the SNES.</p><p>Sets input rate to 32040Hz.</p></body></html>"
msgstr "<html><head/><body><p>When entering fullscreen mode, this option temporarily sets a number of other options to values that make variable refresh rate monitors that use FreeSync or GSync smoother.</p><p>Enables vsync.</p><p>Sets synchronization mode to timer and the frame rate to exactly that of the SNES.</p><p>Sets input rate to 32040Hz.</p></body></html>"

